\section{Propensity callback}{

\code{ml_fitting_propensity(data, predictx)} receives a training data frame with
binary \code{IV} and propensity covariates. \code{predictx} contains only the held-out
propensity covariates, with the same column names; held-out \code{IV} is not
supplied. Return a finite numeric vector of length \code{nrow(predictx)} giving
\code{P(IV = 1 | covariates)}, with values in \verb{[0, 1]}. Return probabilities,
not class labels, logits or a two-column probability matrix.
}

\section{Survival callback}{

\code{ml_fitting_surv(data, predictx)} receives a training list containing \code{time},
\code{event}, \code{Covariates}, \code{Covariates2}, \code{IV}, and \code{D_status}, plus the common
evaluation grid \code{stime}. \code{D_status} has training subjects in rows and grid
times in columns. \code{predictx} is a data frame of held-out survival covariates;
held-out outcomes are not supplied. The grid is shared across folds.

Return a finite numeric matrix with \code{nrow(predictx)} rows and
\code{length(data$stime)} columns, representing the untreated conditional hazard
increments. If \code{H(t | X)} is a predicted cumulative hazard, column 1 is
\code{H(stime[1] | X) - H(0 | X)} and column j is
\code{H(stime[j] | X) - H(stime[j-1] | X)}. Use \code{H(0 | X) = 0}.
Evaluate cumulative hazards on DRIVE's grid first, then difference adjacent
columns. Return the matrix itself, not a list, survival probabilities,
cumulative hazards, or unscaled hazard rates.

By default both learners use \code{dat$Covariates}. Supply \code{dat$Covariates2} for a
different propensity-covariate matrix (or pass \code{Covariates2} through \code{...}).
DRIVE ML returns a scalar treatment effect and its variance; it does not
estimate parametric covariate coefficients. Inspect
\code{fit$DRIVE.ML$Estim$Convergence} after fitting.
The default initial treatment effect is 0.1; \code{Control$init_parameters}
overrides it (the first element is used if a vector is shared with DRIVE joint).
